  "summary": "Structure prediction models now drive virtual screens and iterative design loops for binders, with cell-based and biophysical validation.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the use of predicted structures to rank, filter, or guide the design of protein and peptide binders before experimental testing. Active learning over AlphaFold-based screens of peptide libraries targets BET ET-domain binders, and SEPIA pairs AlphaSeq binding measurements with designed VHH-SEP pseudo-structures to train ML-driven de novo antibody design. An AlphaFold-Multimer virtual screen yields nanobody binders to the GPCR MRGPRX2 that are confirmed in cell assays. Supporting work addresses predictor reliability and scoring: PPIscreenML classifies interacting AF2 pairs against decoys, outperforming pDockQ and iPTM, while a benchmark on 271 protein-peptide complexes finds AF2 more accurate than OpenFold3 and reports evidence of memorization. What is new is the coupling of prediction with iterative design loops and cell-based confirmation of computationally screened binders.",
